  "worked_solution": "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{256{x}^{2}-16}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}\u2061u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{256{x}^{2}-16}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\operatorname{arcosh}\u2061u{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(\\operatorname{arcosh}\u20614-\\operatorname{arcosh}\u20612)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
